{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1782045231187,"sessionID":"ses_115d46e78ffePbz2OLggcEL3CT","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"call_fba627a0210d45be9895e69a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Write pom.xml with Javalin, SQLite, JUnit deps","priority":"high","status":"in_progress"},{"content":"Implement model, repository, controller, main","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Write 5 integration tests","priority":"high","status":"pending"},{"content":"Write README.md","priority":"medium","status":"pending"},{"content":"Build and run tests","priority":"high","status":"pending"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write pom.xml with Javalin, SQLite, JUnit deps\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement model, repository, controller, main\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write 5 integration tests\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write README.md\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Build and run tests\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Write pom.xml with Javalin, SQLite, JUnit deps","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement model, repository, controller, main","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write 5 integration tests","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write README.md","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Build and run tests","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"5 todos","time":{"start":1782045231185,"end":1782045231186}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Java 26 and Maven available. No Gradle. Let me use Maven with a simple setup. \n\nFor Java REST API with SQLite, I have a few choices:\n1. Spring Boot - heavy but standard\n2. Javalin - lightweight, simple, great for this kind of task\n3. Spark Java\n4. Pure com.sun.net.httpserver HttpServer - zero dependencies except SQLite JDBC\n\nSince the task says \"framework\": \"unknown\" and \"tooling\": \"none\", let me pick something that's lightweight and reliable to build. Javalin is a good choice - very simple, clean API, easy to test. Or I could use Spring Boot which is the de facto standard.\n\nLet me go with Javalin because:\n- It's lightweight and easy to set up\n- It has a simple testing story with OkHttp or via its own test utilities\n- SQLite JDBC driver works fine\n- Builds fast (Spring Boot would require more configuration)\n\nActually, let me think about testing. Javalin has good test support. I can use OkHttp as a client, or use Javalin's testing utilities.\n\nFor tests, I need at least 3 unit/integration tests. JUnit 5 with Javalin testing.\n\nLet me plan the structure:\n- pom.xml\n- src/main/java/.../Main.java (or App.java)\n- src/main/java/.../BookController.java\n- src/main/java/.../BookRepository.java (SQLite)\n- src/main/java/.../Book.java (model)\n- src/main/java/.../ValidationException.java or use Javalin's built-in\n- src/main/resources/schema.sql\n- src/test/java/.../BookApiTest.java (3+ tests)\n- README.md\n\nLet me write this. I'll use a package name like `com.example.books`.\n\nDependencies:\n- io.javalin:javalin (latest, maybe 6.x)\n- org.xerial:sqlite-jdbc\n- com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind (javalin includes it but explicitly)\n- org.slf4j:slf4j-simple (javalin needs slf4j)\n- Testing: org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter, com.squareup.okhttp3:okhttp or use Javalin's test approach\n\nLet me check Javalin's latest version. Javalin 6.3.x I believe. Let me check.\n\nActually for testing with Javalin, there's a helper `JavalinTest`. I'll use that.\n\nLet me plan the tests:\n1. Create a book via POST, then GET it back - integration test\n2. Validation: POST with missing title returns 400\n3. Filter by author: GET /books?author=X returns only matching books\n4. DELETE removes the book\n5. Health check returns 200\n\nThat's 5 tests, more than 3.\n\nLet me write everything now. I'll use Java 17+ (records maybe), but Java 26 is available. Let me use Java 21 source level for safety.\n\nActually let me check Javalin's current state. Let me check via web.\n\nActually, I know Javalin 6.x is current. Let me use a known-good version. Javalin 6.3.0 + buddy (Bundle). Javalin 6 requires Java 11+.\n\nLet me write the code. I'll use sqlite-jdbc, the latest is around 3.46ish.\n\nLet me just write the files and then run mvn test to verify.\n\nFor SQLite, I'll create a schema with id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, title TEXT NOT NULL, author TEXT NOT NULL, year INTEGER, isbn TEXT.\n\nRepository will use JDBC directly.\n\nLet me start writing.\n\npom.xml:\n- maven-compiler-plugin with release 21\n- javalin 6.3.0\n- sqlite-jdbc 3.46.1.3  \n- slf4j-simple 2.0.16\n- jackson (javalin brings it)\n- junit-jupiter 5.11.4\n- maven-surefire-plugin\n\nLet me write it. For the app, I'll have:\n- Main.java: configure Javalin, register routes, start server, init DB\n- Book.java: record with id, title, author, year, isbn\n- BookRepository.java: SQLite operations\n- BookController.java: handlers\n- ErrorHandler or use Javalin exception mapping\n\nFor validation: check title and author non-null and non-blank; year optional? Task says title and author required, year and isbn implied optional. I'll require title and author. Year should be a valid integer if provided.\n\nLet me write.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eea2c32e8001Z6U4m5PMU7BxlC","sessionID":"ses_115d46e78ffePbz2OLggcEL3CT","messageID":"msg_eea2bc28c001Hvr2wNnFpUauHh"}}
